              {
                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input has a large square of one color with a smaller square of another color centered inside, on black background. Output is cropped to the large square, colors swapped.\n\nThe verifier: partition by color, find largest object (largest by height*width), remove it, merge the rest (the inner square), subgrid -> crop around inner square... wait, x4 = merge(x3) where x3 = remove(x2, x0). x2 is largest object (the big square). x3 removes it, leaving inner square and background. merge combines remaining cells... but background cells also form object. Hmm.\n\nActually partition groups all cells with same value. So objects: color 0 (background), color 2 (large square), color 1 (small square). Largest by h*w: background might be largest... background is 12x10 = 120 area but bounding box height*width = 12*10=120. Large square: 6*6=36. Inner: 4*4=16. So background is largest -> x2 = background, removed. Then merge remaining = large square + inner square cells. subgrid of that object region = bounding box of both = the large square region (6x6). Then mostcolor = 2 (large square color), leastcolor = 1 (inner). switch swaps 2 and 1. Output = 6x6 with 2->1, 1->2. Yes matches.\n\nSo to create demos, I need input grids with a big colored square containing a smaller colored square centered, on black background.\n\nI need to make 3 distinct examples. Variations: sizes, colors, positions.\n\nNote the output is crop of the large square, so the background outside doesn't matter much, but the large square must be the largest non-background object.\n\nLet me pick:\n\nExample 1: big square 4x4 color 3, inner 2x2 color 5, placed somewhere.\nExample 2: big 5x5 color 4, inner 3x3 color 6... wait inner must be strictly smaller and centered. 5x5 with 1x1 inner centered? Centered means offsets equal. Let's do 5x5 with inner 3x3? then border 1 each side. Yes centered.\n\nActually inner centered in larger: for size n outer and m inner, offset (n-m)/2 must be integer, so n-m even.\n\nExample 3: big 6x6 color 7, inner 2x2 color 8, offset 2.\n\nLet me construct grids ensuring background 0.\n\nExample 1: \nGrid 8x8:\nrows 0-1 all 0\nrows 2-5: big square 4x4 at cols 1-4 color 3, with inner 2x2 at rows 3-4 cols 2-3 color 5.\nLet me write:\nrow0: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\nrow1: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\nrow2: 0 3 3 3 3 0 0 0\nrow3: 0 3 5 5 3 0 0 0\nrow4: 0 3 5 5 3 0 0 0\nrow5: 0 3 3 3 3 0 0 0\nrow6: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\nrow7: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\n\nOutput would be 4x4:\n3 3 3 3 -> 5 5 5 5\n3 5 5 3 -> 5 3 3 5\n3 5 5 3 -> 5 3 3 5\n3 3 3 3 -> 5 5 5 5\n\nExample 2: big 5x5 color 4, inner 3x3 color 6. Place at cols 2-6, rows 2-6 in 9x9 grid.\nrow0: all 0\nrow1: all 0\nrow2: 0 0 4 4 4 4 4 0 0\nrow3: 0 0 4 6 6 6 4 0 0\nrow4: 0 0 4 6 6 6 4 0 0\nrow5: 0 0 4 6 6 6 4 0 0\nrow6: 0 0 4 4 4 4 4 0 0\nrow7: 0...\nrow8: 0...\n\nOutput 5x5: 4s become 6, 6s become 4.\n6 6 6 6 6\n6 4 4 4 6\n6 4 4 4 6\n6 4 4 4 6\n6 6 6 6 6\n\nExample 3: big 6x6 color 7, inner 2x2 color 8, offset 2. Grid maybe 10x10 with big at rows 1-6 cols 3-8.\nrow0: all 0\nrow1: 0 0 0 7 7 7 7 7 7 0\nrow2: 0 0 0 7 7 7 7 7 7 0\nrow3: 0 0 0 7 7 8 8 7 7 0\nrow4: 0 0 0 7 7 8 8 7 7 0\nrow5: 0 0 0 7 7 7 7 7 7 0\nrow6: 0 0 0 7 7 7 7 7 7 0\nrow7: all 0\nrow8: all 0\nrow9: all 0\n\nOutput 6x6:\n7 7 7 7 7 7 -> 8 8 8 8 8 8\n7 7 7 7 7 7 -> 8s\n7 7 8 8 7 7 -> 8 8 7 7 8 8\n7 7 8 8 7 7 -> same\n7 7 7 7 7 7\n7 7 7 7 7 7\n\nWait, need to double check the \"largest object\" is the background. In example 1, background bounding box: background cells are all 0 except the big square region. Background is not contiguous necessarily but partition groups by value regardless. Its bounding box spans entire grid 8x8=64 > 16. Good. Large square 16 > inner 4. So largest = background, removed, then merged large+inner subgrid = big square. Good.\n\nBut wait: what if background forms bounding box = whole grid, that's the largest, always. Then x3 removes background, merge large square and inner. subgrid = bounding box of large square (since inner inside). Correct.\n\nSo any valid input works. Let me submit these three.\n\nActually need to be careful: verifier argmax uses max by height*width. Background 8x8=64. large 16. inner 4. Fine.\n\nIn example 3, grid 10x10, background bbox 100, large 36, inner 4. Fine.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input three times.",
